The Nautilus DevOps team is currently engaged in a cleanup process, focusing on removing unnecessary data and services from their AWS account. 
  As part of the migration process, several resources were created for one-time use only, necessitating a cleanup effort to optimize their AWS environment.

Delete the IAM role named iamrole_kareem using Terraform. Make sure to keep the provisioning code, as we might need to provision this instance again later.

The Terraform working directory is /home/bob/terraform.

Note: Right-click under the EXPLORER section in VS Code and select Open in Integrated Terminal to launch the terminal.

SOLUTION
  To delete the IAM role iamrole_kareem while keeping the provisioning code in main.tf, use Terraform's targeted destroy. This removes the real resource from AWS and its state entry, but leaves the resource block untouched so you can re-provision it later.

📋 Steps
1. Check the resource address in your main.tf:

bash
cd /home/bob/terraform
grep -n -A 5 "iamrole_kareem" main.tf
You'll see something like:

hcl
resource "aws_iam_role" "this" {
  name = "iamrole_kareem"
  ...
}
Note the resource name after aws_iam_role (e.g., this, role, iamrole_kareem). Use that in the next step.

2. Destroy only that IAM role (replace this with your actual resource name):

bash
terraform destroy -target=aws_iam_role.this -auto-approve
This will:

Delete the IAM role iamrole_kareem in AWS.

Remove it from the Terraform state.

Leave the resource "aws_iam_role" ... block in main.tf untouched.

3. Verify the role is deleted:

bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 iam get-role --role-name iamrole_kareem
Expected output:

text
An error occurred (NoSuchEntity) when calling the GetRole operation: The role with name iamrole_kareem cannot be found.
4. Confirm the provisioning code is still present:

bash
grep -n "iamrole_kareem" main.tf
The resource block should still be there.

5. Confirm the resource is no longer in state:

bash
terraform state list
aws_iam_role.<name> should not appear in the list.

⚠️ If the Role Has Attached Policies
IAM roles often have policies attached (e.g., aws_iam_role_policy_attachment or aws_iam_role_policy). If the destroy fails because of these, target-destroy the attachments first:

bash
# Check what's attached in your main.tf
grep -n "iamrole_kareem" main.tf

# Then destroy attachments first, e.g.:
terraform destroy -target=aws_iam_role_policy_attachment.<name> -auto-approve
terraform destroy -target=aws_iam_role.this -auto-approve
💡 Why -target and Not terraform destroy
Command	Effect
terraform destroy -target=aws_iam_role.this	Deletes only the role; main.tf is unchanged ✅
terraform destroy	Destroys all resources ❌
Deleting the block from main.tf	Removes the provisioning code ❌
Once step 3 returns NoSuchEntity and step 4 shows the code is intact, the task is complete.

