      }
    ]
  })

  tags = {
    Name        = "iamrole_kareem"
  }
} main.tf
The user has a main.tf with an aws_iam_role resource named "role". They want to delete it but keep the code. Since it's a bare role with no attached policies, a single targeted destroy should work.

Since your resource is named role (i.e., resource "aws_iam_role" "role"), the correct target address is aws_iam_role.role. This role has no attached policies, so a single targeted destroy is all you need.

📋 Steps
1. Destroy only the IAM role (keeps the code in main.tf):

bash
cd /home/bob/terraform
terraform destroy -target=aws_iam_role.role -auto-approve
This will:

Delete the IAM role iamrole_kareem in AWS.

Remove it from the Terraform state.

Leave the resource "aws_iam_role" "role" { ... } block in main.tf untouched, so you can re-provision it later.

2. Verify the role is deleted:

bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 iam get-role --role-name iamrole_kareem
Expected output:

text
An error occurred (NoSuchEntity) when calling the GetRole operation: The role with name iamrole_kareem cannot be found.
3. Confirm the provisioning code is still present:

bash
grep -n "iamrole_kareem" main.tf
You should still see your original block:

hcl
resource "aws_iam_role" "role" {
  name = "iamrole_kareem"
  ...
}
4. Confirm the resource is no longer in state:

bash
terraform state list
aws_iam_role.role should not appear in the list.

💡 Why -target=aws_iam_role.role
Part	Meaning
aws_iam_role	The resource type
role	The resource name (the label after the type in your main.tf)
The full address is always <type>.<name> — so in your case, aws_iam_role.role.

⚠️ If the Destroy Fails
Your role has only an assume_role_policy (the trust policy), which is inherent to the role and doesn't block deletion. Since there are no aws_iam_role_policy_attachment or aws_iam_role_policy resources in your main.tf, the single command above should succeed cleanly.

Once step 2 returns NoSuchEntity and step 3 shows the code is intact, the task is complete.
  
